The "self-stimulation" framing and its formal control-theoretic treatment is a genuinely fresh lens for time series forecasting. Reinterpreting the forecasting stagnation problem as a mathematical lower bound imposed by ignoring exogenous inputs is a nice conceptual contribution. However, the underlying idea—using external signals to improve forecasting—is not new; prior work on multimodal TSF, LLM-augmented forecasting, and exogenous variable modeling exists. The novelty lies primarily in the *theoretical justification* and the *operationalization* (leak-free benchmark, LLM-free model). This places it above `lamda_benchmark` (engineering novelty) in conceptual novelty, but below `distributional_equivalence` (truly foundational theory) or `neural_posterior_estimation` (algorithmic novelty). **~Moderate-good.**

