Its main weaknesses align with calibration papers in the 4.5-5.3 range: single-run RL experiments without significance testing, limited policy model scale (7B only), and a predominantly phenomenological rather than mechanistic treatment. The topic is more timely and focused than the "math reasoning data" paper (avg 4.0), and the empirical breadth is comparable to the Fano paper (avg 5.3) or LRT paper (avg 5.0). The lack of a strong constructive method (the hybrid verifier is promising but preliminary) keeps it from exceeding the borderline-accept threshold clearly.

