### Dimension-by-Dimension Comparison vs. the Paper Under Review

**Novelty:** High. The paper introduces the first unified, algorithm-agnostic definition of forgetting across all learning paradigms, cleanly disentangling it from backward transfer. This is genuinely a first. It sits close to the distributional_equivalence paper (first solution to an open problem) in conceptual novelty, but the insight is more definitional/conceptual than technically deep (no breakthrough mathematical tools like edge ranks or matroid duality).

**Technical soundness:** Moderate. The framework is principled (grounded in predictive self-consistency, KL divergence, particle-based rollouts) and logically coherent. However, it lacks the mathematical rigor of the 8.0-scoring distributional_equivalence paper or even the 7.5-scoring ReLU paper. Critical design choices (divergence D, horizon k, number of particles) have no sensitivity analysis, weakening soundness.

