**Significance:** High. Understanding the relationship between adaptive methods and NSD is central to modern optimization theory, especially given the recent practical success of methods like Muon/Lion. The concrete separation results (acceleration under adaptive smoothness, dimension-free rates under adaptive variance) have clear theoretical value. Comparable to "Attention head count" (7.0) and "Rate separations" (6.7).

**Clarity:** The review notes the paper is necessarily technical but could benefit from more accessible exposition. The weaknesses are moderate: questions about when adaptive smoothness differs from standard smoothness in practice, limited scope of the lower bound (only ℓ∞), and unclear tightness of log factors.

