**Novelty**: The paper introduces a new problem formulation (single-node conditional-intervention causal bandits) and provides a graphical characterization (mGISS via Λ-structures) with a linear-time algorithm (C4). The equivalence between conditional and deterministic atomic intervention superiority (Proposition 4) is an elegant insight. However, this is fundamentally an extension of Lee & Bareinboim (2018) to a different setting (single-node, conditional, no latent confounders). The novelty is solid but somewhat incremental within the causal bandits literature.

**Technical soundness**: Proofs appear rigorous. The Λ-structure characterization and O(|V|+|E|) algorithm are clean results. The theoretical machinery is well-constructed but not at the level of difficulty or depth seen in top-scoring calibration papers.

**Empirical support**: This is the paper's weakest dimension. Synthetic graphs use only Erdős-Rényi models. Bandit experiments lack comparison to natural baselines (e.g., intervening only on parents of Y). Key experimental details are sparse in the main text. The evaluation doesn't convincingly demonstrate the practical utility of the full mGISS characterization beyond brute-force search.

**Significance**: The contribution is a preprocessing step for causal MAB problems. The assumption of no latent confounders is a significant limitation for real-world applicability. The restricted scope (single-node, causal sufficiency) limits the breadth of impact, though the authors reasonably argue this is a necessary first step.

**Clarity**: Well-written with clear motivation and problem formulation.

