## Comparative Analysis

### Paper Summary
This paper identifies three misconceptions in the online map-based motion prediction protocol for autonomous driving: (1) a train-val gap from inappropriate data splits, (2) a range mismatch between mapping and prediction models, and (3) non-discriminative evaluation metrics. It proposes OMMP-Bench with a spatially-disjoint 3-way split, refined evaluation on moving non-ego agents, and an image-feature baseline for out-of-range agents.

### Dimension-by-Dimension Comparison

**Novelty (Moderate)**
The individual issues (spatial overlaps in nuScenes, limited map range) have been partially noted in prior work. The paper's novelty lies in the holistic synthesis of these issues and the benchmark formalization. The image-feature baseline is simple (deformable attention on image features). Compare to GAIA2 (8.0), which introduced a fundamentally new platform for asynchronous agent evaluation, or LAMDA (6.7), which created an unprecedented-scale longitudinal dataset. This paper's novelty is more incremental—it's a correction/improvement of an existing protocol rather than a new paradigm.

**Technical Soundness (Good)**
The diagnosis is clearly evidence-backed. Table 1 convincingly shows the train-val gap. Table 2 shows map accuracy drops at longer range. Table 6 shows metric inflation from static/ego-only evaluation. The three-way split and evaluation refinements are well-motivated. No major technical flaws. Comparable to LAMDA (6.7) in soundness, better than MermaidFlow (5.3) which had untested claims about guarantees.

**Empirical Support (Adequate but Limited)**
All experiments are on nuScenes only—the only viable dataset, but this limits demonstrated generality. Only 2 motion prediction models (HiVT, DenseTNT) and 2 map models (MapTR, MapTRv2-CL) are tested. The image-feature baseline shows consistent improvements (Table 7) but lacks mechanistic analysis. Compare to GAIA2 (8.0), which evaluated dozens of models across multiple capability dimensions, or LAMDA (6.7), which included 12 years of data with multifaceted validation. This paper's empirical scope is narrower.

**Significance (Moderate)**
Important for the specific subfield of online map-based motion prediction, but the audience is narrow. The corrections are necessary and practical, but the paper is more about "fixing what's broken" than opening new directions. Compare to GAIA2 (8.0), which established an entirely new evaluation infrastructure with broad community impact. This paper's impact is confined to practitioners working on this specific protocol.

**Clarity (Good)**
Well-written with clear problem articulation, good figures (especially the split comparison in Fig. 4), and well-structured experiments. Table formatting has some issues (Col2, Col3 placeholders in Table 7). Comparable to or slightly better than most calibration papers in this range.

### Positioning Against Calibration Papers

